{"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"","title":"Home","text":""},{"location":"#llm-tutors-for-all","title":"LLM Tutors For All","text":"<p>An applied research and discovery project exploring practical ways to deploy LLM-powered tutors, with a focus on performance and cost tradeoffs across services. We are developing reproducible benchmark harnesses to test tutor models under consistent conditions.</p> <p>Explore tutor options View benchmarks</p>"},{"location":"#making-llm-tutors-easier-to-understand","title":"Making LLM tutors easier to understand","text":"<p>There is no single best way to deploy an LLM tutor. The right approach depends on where learners work, how quickly and reliably the tutor responds, and what it costs to operate. LLM Tutors For All treats each implementation as both a usable tutor and a research case study. The goal is to document working deployment patterns and compare them through practical, repeatable benchmarks.</p>"},{"location":"#what-we-currently-use","title":"What we currently use","text":"<ul> <li>Course material: the UC Berkeley Data 8 Spring 2026 corpus</li> <li>Tutor design: retrieval-augmented generation grounded in course content</li> <li>Retrieval: a local BM25 index, with optional provider-hosted embeddings</li> <li>Model service: OpenRouter, using <code>openai/gpt-4o-mini</code> by default and   supporting other selectable models</li> <li>Interfaces: a local command-line tutor, Discord, Slack, and a separately   hosted Grok Bot template</li> </ul> <p>The self-hosted interfaces use one tutoring engine, while Grok Bot keeps its own corpus on a cloud computer. Documenting both patterns lets the project study how service and deployment choices affect latency, reliability, usage, operating cost, and the learner experience.</p>"},{"location":"#research-direction","title":"Research direction","text":"<p>The current Data 8 GPT corpus and OpenRouter brain are the first framework combination, not the intended limit of the project. Tutor interfaces and framework components are documented separately so that future course corpora and model brains can be added without restructuring the site.</p> <p>Benchmark work will compare only options that have been implemented, rather than assume that one approach is universally best. Results will include the methodology, harness configuration, test questions, and measured data as experiments are completed. This makes model comparisons repeatable instead of depending on isolated demonstrations or impressions.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/","title":"Benchmarking LLM tutors","text":"<p>Choosing an educational model requires more than checking whether it can produce the right final answer. A useful tutor should recognize where a learner went wrong, offer an appropriate next step, avoid doing the work for them, and remain practical to deploy.</p> <p>LLM Tutors For All is building a reproducible benchmark for comparing both language models and, later, complete tutor systems under consistent conditions. The goal is to help instructors and developers understand the tradeoffs among teaching quality, cost, speed, and deployment constraints.</p> <p>Work in progress</p> <p>The benchmark platform is under active development. This section describes the current scope and methodology; comparative results and recommendations will be published after the evaluation design and judges have been calibrated.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/#what-the-benchmark-asks","title":"What the benchmark asks","text":"<p>The project studies questions such as:</p> <ul> <li>Does the tutor identify the learner's mistake and locate it accurately?</li> <li>Does it provide correct, relevant, and actionable guidance?</li> <li>Does it guide the learner without revealing the final answer?</li> <li>Is its response coherent, natural, and appropriately encouraging?</li> <li>How much does each response cost, and how quickly is it produced?</li> <li>Do conclusions remain stable across repeated runs and different evaluators?</li> </ul> <p>These questions are evaluated separately. A model that is inexpensive or fast may not be the strongest pedagogical choice, while a high-quality response may be impractical at scale. The benchmark will report these tradeoffs rather than collapse them into a single \"best model\" score.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/#what-is-being-compared","title":"What is being compared","text":""},{"location":"benchmark/#models","title":"Models","text":"<p>The initial work compares proprietary and open-weight language models accessed through a common evaluation harness. This includes models served by commercial APIs as well as models available through shared or self-hosted infrastructure. Each model receives the same tasks and tutoring instructions.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/#tutor-systems","title":"Tutor systems","text":"<p>A tutor is more than its underlying model. System prompts, retrieval, course context, guardrails, and orchestration can all change the learner experience. The platform includes a common adapter design so complete tutor systems can eventually be tested with the same tasks and scoring methods as raw models.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/#interactive-settings","title":"Interactive settings","text":"<p>The project is also preparing evaluations with simulated students, including multi-turn tutoring and office-hours-style queues. This work will examine answer leakage, responsiveness, attention allocation, wait time, and starvation. Simulation results will only be used after the simulated students pass explicit validity checks.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/#what-a-benchmark-run-produces","title":"What a benchmark run produces","text":"<p>For each task, the harness records the full prompt and response, model and configuration, token use, timing, and scoring details. Responses can then be rescored without generating them again.</p> <p>Reports are designed to include:</p> <ul> <li>pedagogical quality by evaluation dimension</li> <li>answer-leakage and response-length checks</li> <li>agreement between automated judges and human labels</li> <li>pairwise model preferences and judge consistency</li> <li>median and 95th-percentile latency</li> <li>token usage and estimated cost</li> <li>uncertainty intervals across repeated samples</li> </ul> <p>Read How evaluation works for the scoring process, reliability safeguards, and current development status.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/#project-resources","title":"Project resources","text":"<ul> <li>Benchmark repository</li> <li>Inspect AI, the evaluation framework used by   the project</li> <li>Model options currently documented on this site</li> </ul>"},{"location":"contact/","title":"Contact","text":""},{"location":"contact/#contact","title":"Contact","text":"<p>Questions about LLM Tutors For All? Meet the team and explore the project links below.</p>"},{"location":"contact/#meet-the-team","title":"Meet the teamBenjamin TelanoffChristopher MachEdwin VargasJustin LeKhas-ErdeneLindsay LaiSilas SantiniYiran Hu","text":"<p>Project contributor</p> <p> GitHub LinkedIn </p> <p>Benchmark Researcher</p> <p> GitHub </p> <p>Project Manager</p> <p> GitHub LinkedIn </p> <p>Benchmark Researcher</p> <p> GitHub </p> <p>Benchmark Researcher</p> <p> GitHub </p> <p>Benchmark Researcher</p> <p> GitHub </p> <p>Project Manager</p> <p> GitHub LinkedIn </p> <p>Benchmark Researcher</p> <p> GitHub </p>"},{"location":"contact/#project-links","title":"Project links","text":"<ul> <li>LLM Tutors For All on GitHub</li> <li>Documentation repository</li> </ul>"},{"location":"benchmark/evaluation/","title":"How evaluation works","text":"<p>The benchmark separates response generation from scoring. This makes model comparisons repeatable and allows new scoring methods to be applied to existing responses without paying to generate them again.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/evaluation/#evaluation-pipeline","title":"Evaluation pipeline","text":"<ol> <li>Build representative tasks. Items contain the conversation so far, the    educational problem, a private reference answer or solution, and any    item-specific rubric.</li> <li>Generate tutor responses. Candidate models receive the same task and    tutoring prompt. Multiple samples are collected because hosted models are    not perfectly deterministic.</li> <li>Run inexpensive checks. Automated checks measure properties such as    response length and possible answer leakage.</li> <li>Score teaching quality. Calibrated LLM judges evaluate each response    against a pedagogical rubric. Pairwise judging can also compare two    responses directly.</li> <li>Analyze the run. Reports combine quality scores with token use, estimated    cost, latency, sample counts, and uncertainty intervals.</li> </ol> <p>The complete prompts, responses, settings, usage, and timing are retained in the evaluation logs so that published numbers can be traced back to their source.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/evaluation/#pedagogical-dimensions","title":"Pedagogical dimensions","text":"<p>The initial rubric follows the eight dimensions used by MRBench:</p> <ul> <li>Mistake identification: Did the tutor recognize an error?</li> <li>Mistake location: Did it accurately point to where the error occurred?</li> <li>Answer revealing: Did it avoid giving away the final answer?</li> <li>Guidance: Did it provide correct and relevant help?</li> <li>Actionability: Is the learner's next step clear?</li> <li>Coherence: Does the response fit the preceding conversation?</li> <li>Tone: Is the response encouraging, neutral, or offensive?</li> <li>Human-likeness: Does the response sound natural rather than robotic?</li> </ul> <p>The categorical labels and their distributions remain part of the report. Numeric summaries are useful for comparison, but they do not replace the underlying rubric.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/evaluation/#more-than-one-kind-of-evaluator","title":"More than one kind of evaluator","text":"<p>No single automated judge is assumed to be correct. The platform supports several complementary approaches:</p> <ul> <li>Rubric judging scores one response across the pedagogical dimensions.</li> <li>Cross-provider juries combine judges from different model vendors.</li> <li>Pairwise judging compares two responses in both presentation orders.</li> <li>Human ratings provide an external reference for automated judges.</li> <li>Calibration datasets test judges against existing expert labels from   MRBench and the BEA 2025 shared task.</li> </ul> <p>Judge quality is measured with agreement statistics such as Cohen's kappa, not only raw agreement. Pairwise comparisons swap response order to expose position bias, and self-judging experiments check whether a model favors responses from its own provider.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/evaluation/#reliability-and-fair-comparison","title":"Reliability and fair comparison","text":"<p>The methodology includes safeguards intended to prevent a clean-looking leaderboard from hiding unstable results:</p> <ul> <li>run each item multiple times and retain raw responses</li> <li>pin model versions and record generation settings where providers allow it</li> <li>keep reference answers away from the tutor while making them available to   evaluators</li> <li>report answer leakage separately from general response quality</li> <li>use clustered bootstrap confidence intervals for repeated conversations</li> <li>report latency separately as median and 95th percentile</li> <li>publish judge parsing failures, pairwise inconsistencies, and excluded runs</li> </ul> <p>Latency reflects a service at a particular time, not only the model itself. Likewise, automated pedagogical scores are meaningful only when the judge has shown adequate agreement with human labels.</p>"},{"location":"benchmark/evaluation/#current-status","title":"Current status","text":"<p>As of September 2026, the baseline evaluation platform can generate and cache responses, run rubric and pairwise scoring, calibrate judges, and report quality, cost, and latency. The first pilot uses Data 8 dialogue tasks.</p> <p>The team is currently calibrating judges and expanding the pilot dataset before publishing model comparisons. Human-rating, simulated-student, office-hours queue, and complete tutor-adapter evaluations are planned work rather than finished results.</p> <p>How to interpret this section</p> <p>Until result pages are published, descriptions here explain what the framework is designed to measure. They should not be read as claims that one model or tutor has already outperformed another.</p> <p>Return to the benchmark overview</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/","title":"How to Use Our Bots","text":"<p>This section contains the complete setup and usage instructions for every course bot in LLM Tutors For All.</p> <p>As bots for additional classes are added, each class will have its own area in the left navigation. That area will explain how to prepare the course tutor, configure its required services, start it, and verify that it is working.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/#current-bots","title":"Current bots","text":"Data 8 <p>Set up a tutor grounded in UC Berkeley Data 8 Spring 2026 course material.</p> <p>New course bots can be added to this gallery with their own setup guides, corpora, and service requirements.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/","title":"Data 8 Bot: Getting Started","text":"<p>This guide prepares and runs the self-hosted Data 8 tutor. Complete it before starting the local, Discord, or Slack option. The hosted Grok Bot setup uses a shared template that sets up the Spring 2026 materials on first use.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#current-course","title":"Current course","text":"<p>The tutor is grounded in UC Berkeley Data 8 Spring 2026 lectures, assignments, textbook material, worksheets, and exams.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#how-it-works","title":"How it works","text":""},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#course-corpus","title":"Course corpus","text":"<p>Data 8 lectures, assignments, and references provide the tutor's grounded knowledge.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#local-retrieval","title":"Local retrieval","text":"<p>BM25, with optional embeddings, selects course passages related to each question.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#tutor-brain","title":"Tutor brain","text":"<p>An OpenRouter model uses retrieved context to construct a focused answer. You can select a different supported model without changing the tutor code.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#tutor-interfaces","title":"Tutor interfaces","text":"<p>The same response engine supports the local CLI, Discord, and Slack bots.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#requirements","title":"Requirements","text":"<ul> <li>Python 3</li> <li>A clone of the   LLM-Tutors-For-All repository</li> <li>An OpenRouter API key for generated answers</li> </ul>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#1-create-the-environment","title":"1. Create the environment","text":"<p>Open a terminal in the project repository:</p> Windows PowerShellmacOS or Linux <p><code>powershell python -m venv .venv .\\.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 pip install -r requirements.txt Copy-Item .env.example .env</code></p> <p><code>bash python -m venv .venv source .venv/bin/activate pip install -r requirements.txt cp .env.example .env</code></p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#2-configure-the-model","title":"2. Configure the model","text":"<p>Open <code>.env</code>, keep OpenRouter as the provider, and add your API key:</p> <pre><code>LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter\nOPENROUTER_API_KEY=your-api-key\nOPENROUTER_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini\nOPENROUTER_APP_NAME=LLM Tutors For All\n</code></pre> <p><code>OPENROUTER_MODEL</code> selects the model used by the tutor. The default is <code>openai/gpt-4o-mini</code>, but it can be replaced with another model identifier from the OpenRouter model directory.</p> <p>The following optional settings are also available:</p> <pre><code># OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small\n# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1\n# OPENROUTER_SITE_URL=https://your-app.example\n</code></pre> <p><code>OPENROUTER_SITE_URL</code> identifies your deployment to OpenRouter. OpenAI remains available as a fallback by setting <code>LLM_PROVIDER=openai</code> and configuring the <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>, <code>OPENAI_MODEL</code>, and optional OpenAI settings included in <code>.env.example</code>.</p> <p>Keep credentials private</p> <p>Never commit <code>.env</code> or paste API and bot tokens into documentation, issues, or chat messages.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#3-build-the-local-index","title":"3. Build the local index","text":"<pre><code>python -m tutor ingest\npython -m tutor status\n</code></pre> <p>The default index uses local BM25 retrieval and does not need an API key. To add provider-hosted embeddings and combine them with BM25 retrieval:</p> <pre><code>python -m tutor ingest --embed\n</code></pre> <p>When using OpenRouter, configure <code>OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_MODEL</code> before running this command. The OpenAI fallback instead uses <code>OPENAI_EMBEDDING_MODEL</code>.</p> <p>Rebuild the index whenever course files in <code>gpt_corpus/</code> change.</p>"},{"location":"how-to-use-our-bots/data8/getting-started/#4-verify-retrieval-and-answers","title":"4. Verify retrieval and answers","text":"<p>Check retrieval without calling the LLM:</p> <pre><code>python -m tutor retrieve \"How do I filter rows of a table?\"\n</code></pre> <p>Then ask a generated question:</p> <pre><code>python -m tutor ask \"I'm stuck on using tbl.where\"\n</code></pre> <p>Continue with the local interactive tutor, the Discord integration, or the Slack integration. For a hosted alternative, follow the Grok Bot tutor guide.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/","title":"Model Options","text":"<p>LLM Tutors For All separates each tutor's knowledge base from its brain so that course information can be evaluated independently from the model that generates responses.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/#knowledge-base-versus-brain","title":"Knowledge base versus brain","text":"<ul> <li>The knowledge base, or corpus, contains the course information available   to the tutor. Its local search index retrieves relevant material for each   question.</li> <li>The brain is the configured language model. It receives the question,   tutoring instructions, and retrieved course context, then generates the   response.</li> </ul> <p>Keeping them separate makes it possible to compare course corpora, retrieval approaches, and model providers in controlled combinations.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/#data-8-options","title":"Data 8 options","text":"<p>The Data 8 tutor combines its course corpus with one configured model provider:</p> <ul> <li>GPT Corpus: a local collection and search index   of Data 8 Spring 2026 course material acting as the knowledge base</li> <li>OpenRouter Brain: a selectable model   accessed through OpenRouter that generates answers from retrieved course   context; this is the recommended provider</li> <li>OpenAI Brain: direct access to an OpenAI model,   retained as an alternative provider</li> </ul> <p>The corpus and selected brain form the tutor's retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG, pipeline. OpenRouter lets the same tutor compare or adopt models from different developers, while the OpenAI option documents the direct-provider configuration.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/corpus-options/","title":"Data 8 GPT Corpus","text":"<p>The GPT corpus is the local knowledge component of the Data 8 tutor. It grounds answers in UC Berkeley Data 8 Spring 2026 course material instead of asking the model to rely only on its pretrained knowledge.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/corpus-options/#what-it-contains","title":"What it contains","text":"<p>The Markdown corpus under <code>gpt_corpus/data8/sp26/</code> includes:</p> <ul> <li>Lectures</li> <li>Assignments and projects</li> <li>Textbook material</li> <li>Discussion and tutoring worksheets</li> <li>Exams</li> </ul> <p>Student mode excludes solution-only files and strips solution sections from student-facing material.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/corpus-options/#how-indexing-works","title":"How indexing works","text":"<pre><code>python -m tutor ingest\n</code></pre> <p>The ingestion process splits the course files into chunks of up to roughly 3,500 characters and stores a local BM25 index under <code>tutor_data/</code>. BM25 ranks chunks by how well their terms match a student's question.</p> <p>The default index:</p> <ul> <li>Runs locally</li> <li>Does not require an API key</li> <li>Does not create retrieval API charges</li> <li>Can be inspected with <code>python -m tutor retrieve \"question\"</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/corpus-options/#optional-embeddings","title":"Optional embeddings","text":"<pre><code>python -m tutor ingest --embed\n</code></pre> <p>This embeds the corpus with the embedding model configured for the selected provider. With the recommended OpenRouter setup, set <code>OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_MODEL</code> before running the command. Each question is also embedded, and its semantic ranking is combined with BM25.</p> <p>Embedding-assisted retrieval can find conceptually similar passages when the question and course material use different words. It also adds an initial corpus-embedding cost and an embedding request for each new question.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/corpus-options/#advantages","title":"Advantages","text":"<ul> <li>Keeps the source course material local</li> <li>Grounds answers in identifiable course files</li> <li>Avoids hosted vector-database infrastructure</li> <li>Works with free local BM25 retrieval by default</li> <li>Separates student and staff content</li> <li>Can be rebuilt when course material changes</li> </ul>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/corpus-options/#limitations","title":"Limitations","text":"<ul> <li>Exact-term BM25 retrieval may miss conceptually similar wording</li> <li>Embeddings improve semantic matching but add API cost</li> <li>Corpus quality depends on the completeness and conversion of source material</li> <li>The local index must be rebuilt after course files change</li> <li>This corpus currently covers only Data 8 Spring 2026</li> </ul>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/corpus-options/#rag-versus-the-local-corpus","title":"RAG versus the local corpus","text":"<p>The corpus and RAG are not alternatives:</p> <ol> <li>The corpus stores the course knowledge.</li> <li>Retrieval selects relevant chunks using BM25 and optional embeddings.</li> <li>The configured OpenRouter model generates an answer from those chunks.</li> <li>The complete retrieve-then-generate process is RAG.</li> </ol> <p>The default setup is still RAG even though its corpus and BM25 index are local.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openai-options/","title":"Data 8 OpenAI Brain","text":"<p>The Data 8 tutor supports direct OpenAI access as an alternative to the recommended OpenRouter provider. It uses the OpenAI Chat Completions API to generate responses from retrieved course context. Its default OpenAI model is <code>gpt-4o-mini</code>.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openai-options/#configuration","title":"Configuration","text":"<p>Create a key on the OpenAI API key page and add it to the project's <code>.env</code> file:</p> <pre><code>LLM_PROVIDER=openai\nOPENAI_API_KEY=your-api-key\nOPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini\n</code></pre> <p>The API key is separate from a ChatGPT subscription. Keep it private and never commit <code>.env</code>.</p> <p><code>OPENAI_MODEL</code> is optional. The project also accepts <code>OPENAI_BASE_URL</code> for an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openai-options/#how-the-brain-is-used","title":"How the brain is used","text":"<p>For each question, the tutor:</p> <ol> <li>Retrieves up to eight relevant course chunks by default.</li> <li>Combines them with tutor instructions, the question, and recent chat    history.</li> <li>Sends that input to the configured model.</li> <li>Returns the generated answer and course source files.</li> </ol> <p>The local CLI, Discord, and Slack tutor options all use this same brain.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openai-options/#token-use","title":"Token use","text":"<p>Input tokens include:</p> <ul> <li>Tutor system instructions</li> <li>Retrieved course chunks</li> <li>The student's question</li> <li>Recent conversation history</li> </ul> <p>Output tokens are the generated response. Longer context and conversations increase cost.</p> <p>The current code does not save OpenAI's returned <code>usage</code> values, so measured per-question token totals are not available yet.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openai-options/#current-cost","title":"Current cost","text":"<p>OpenAI lists the standard text rates for <code>gpt-4o-mini</code> as:</p> <ul> <li>Input: $0.15 per 1 million tokens</li> <li>Cached input: $0.075 per 1 million tokens</li> <li>Output: $0.60 per 1 million tokens</li> </ul> <p>The optional <code>text-embedding-3-small</code> retrieval model is listed at $0.02 per 1 million input tokens.</p> <p>Prices were checked on September 9, 2026 and may change. Verify the official model pages before reporting benchmark costs.</p> <p>For illustration, 8,000 input tokens and 500 output tokens at these <code>gpt-4o-mini</code> rates would cost about $0.0015. This is an example, not a measurement of the Data 8 tutor.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openai-options/#advantages","title":"Advantages","text":"<ul> <li>Low listed token price for the default model</li> <li>Simple hosted API with no local model infrastructure</li> <li>Shared implementation across local, Discord, and Slack tutors</li> <li>Model can be changed through an environment variable</li> <li>Supports an OpenAI-compatible custom endpoint</li> </ul>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openai-options/#limitations","title":"Limitations","text":"<ul> <li>Requires an external service and API key</li> <li>Usage creates variable operating cost</li> <li>Availability and latency depend on the provider</li> <li>Course context and questions are sent to the configured API</li> <li>Pricing and model behavior can change</li> <li>The current tutor does not record token usage for benchmarking</li> <li>Fully local generation is not currently implemented</li> </ul>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openrouter-options/","title":"Data 8 OpenRouter Brain","text":"<p>The Data 8 tutor uses OpenRouter as its recommended model provider. OpenRouter exposes models from multiple developers through one API, so the tutor's model can be changed without replacing its response pipeline. The default model is <code>openai/gpt-4o-mini</code>.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openrouter-options/#configuration","title":"Configuration","text":"<p>Create an API key in your OpenRouter account, then configure the project's <code>.env</code> file:</p> <pre><code>LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter\nOPENROUTER_API_KEY=your-api-key\nOPENROUTER_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini\nOPENROUTER_APP_NAME=LLM Tutors For All\n</code></pre> <p>Keep the API key private and never commit <code>.env</code>.</p> <p><code>OPENROUTER_MODEL</code> can be set to another identifier from the OpenRouter model directory. Model capabilities, context limits, availability, and pricing vary, so verify that a model supports the tutor's requirements before selecting it.</p> <p>Optional settings include:</p> <pre><code># OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small\n# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1\n# OPENROUTER_SITE_URL=https://your-app.example\n</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_MODEL</code> selects a model for optional semantic retrieval.</li> <li><code>OPENROUTER_BASE_URL</code> overrides the API endpoint.</li> <li><code>OPENROUTER_SITE_URL</code> identifies the deployment to OpenRouter.</li> <li><code>OPENROUTER_APP_NAME</code> supplies the application name shown to OpenRouter.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openrouter-options/#how-the-brain-is-used","title":"How the brain is used","text":"<p>For each question, the tutor:</p> <ol> <li>Retrieves relevant course chunks from the local Data 8 index.</li> <li>Combines them with tutor instructions, the question, and recent chat    history.</li> <li>Sends that input to the configured OpenRouter model.</li> <li>Returns the generated answer and relevant course sources.</li> </ol> <p>The local CLI, Discord, and Slack tutor options all use this same configured brain.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openrouter-options/#changing-models","title":"Changing models","text":"<p>Only the model identifier needs to change:</p> <pre><code>OPENROUTER_MODEL=provider/model-name\n</code></pre> <p>Restart a running tutor after changing <code>.env</code>. Using one provider interface makes it easier to test models under the same tutor prompt, retrieval process, and application code.</p> <p>Changing models can still affect answer quality, context capacity, latency, availability, and cost. A model name should therefore be recorded alongside any benchmark or deployment result.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openrouter-options/#model-costs","title":"Model costs","text":"<p>OpenRouter charges separately for input and output tokens. Input includes the tutor instructions, retrieved course passages, the student's question, and recent conversation history. Output is the model's generated response and, for some models, may also include billed reasoning tokens.</p> <p>The following selection includes four of OpenRouter's most-used models by token volume on October 6, 2026, plus this project's default model. Popularity measures usage through OpenRouter; it does not measure tutoring quality.</p> <ul> <li>DeepSeek V4.1 Flash   (<code>deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash</code>): $0.045 per million input tokens and   $1.20 per million output tokens</li> <li>GLM 5.3 Flash   (<code>z-ai/glm-5.3-flash</code>): $0.0214 per million input tokens and $0.50 per   million output tokens</li> <li>MiMo-V2.6-Flash   (<code>xiaomi/mimo-v2.6-flash</code>): $0.10 per million input tokens and $0.28   per million output tokens</li> <li>GPT-6 Luna   (<code>openai/gpt-6-luna</code>): $0.10 per million input tokens and $0.50 per   million output tokens</li> <li>GPT-4o mini   (<code>openai/gpt-4o-mini</code>, the current default): $0.15 per million input   tokens and $0.60 per million output tokens</li> </ul> <p>For illustration, a turn containing 8,000 input tokens and 500 output tokens would cost approximately:</p> <ul> <li>DeepSeek V4.1 Flash: $0.00096</li> <li>GLM 5.3 Flash: $0.00042</li> <li>MiMo-V2.6-Flash: $0.00094</li> <li>GPT-6 Luna: $0.00105</li> <li>GPT-4o mini: $0.00150</li> </ul> <p>These are estimates based on the listed text-token rates, checked October 7, 2026. They exclude cache pricing, embeddings, web search, provider-routing preferences, and other model-specific charges. OpenRouter rankings and prices change over time, so check the linked model pages before budgeting or reporting benchmark costs.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openrouter-options/#optional-embeddings","title":"Optional embeddings","text":"<p>The default BM25 index is local and does not need an API key. To add semantic retrieval, select an embedding model and rebuild the index:</p> <pre><code>OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small\n</code></pre> <pre><code>python -m tutor ingest --embed\n</code></pre> <p>Embedding requests have their own availability and pricing. Check the selected model's OpenRouter page before use.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openrouter-options/#openai-fallback","title":"OpenAI fallback","text":"<p>Direct OpenAI access remains supported:</p> <pre><code>LLM_PROVIDER=openai\nOPENAI_API_KEY=your-api-key\nOPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini\n# OPENAI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small\n# OPENAI_BASE_URL=\n</code></pre> <p>Use one provider configuration at a time. An OpenRouter key cannot be used as an OpenAI key, and a ChatGPT subscription does not include API access.</p>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openrouter-options/#advantages","title":"Advantages","text":"<ul> <li>One API configuration can access models from multiple developers</li> <li>Models can be changed without modifying tutor code</li> <li>No local model-serving infrastructure is required</li> <li>The same provider configuration is shared by all tutor interfaces</li> <li>Direct OpenAI access remains available as a fallback</li> </ul>"},{"location":"model-options/data8/openrouter-options/#limitations","title":"Limitations","text":"<ul> <li>Generated answers require an external provider and API key</li> <li>Price, context limits, and capabilities differ by selected model</li> <li>Model availability and routing can change</li> <li>Latency depends on OpenRouter and the underlying model provider</li> <li>Course context and student questions are sent to external services</li> <li>Fully local generation is not currently implemented</li> </ul>"},{"location":"tutor-options/","title":"Tutor Options","text":"<p>The project offers one Data 8 tutor through three self-hosted interfaces and a Grok Bot template. The self-hosted options share the same course corpus, retrieval system, and tutoring engine; Grok Bot keeps its own corpus on a persistent cloud computer.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/#choose-an-interface","title":"Choose an interface","text":"Local CLI <p>Run one-shot questions, interactive chat, retrieval checks, and staff mode.</p> Discord <p>Let students ask through mentions, direct messages, or the tutor command.</p> Slack <p>Use mentions, direct messages, or the tutor command through Socket Mode.</p> Grok Bot <p>Add the hosted template. On first use it sets up the Spring 2026 materials on its own.</p> <p>The local CLI, Discord, and Slack options currently use the same Data 8 course corpus and OpenRouter brain. The configured OpenRouter model can be changed without changing those interfaces or their retrieval pipeline. Grok Bot is a separate hosted option with its own plan, usage, storage, and privacy terms.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/","title":"Discord setup","text":"<p>The Discord tutor responds to mentions, direct messages, and the <code>/tutor</code> slash command. Complete Data 8 Getting Started first.</p> <p></p> <p></p> <p></p> <p></p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#create-the-app","title":"Create the app","text":"<p>Register a Discord application and create the tutor's bot identity.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#configure-access","title":"Configure access","text":"<p>Store the bot token privately and enable only the required message intent.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#install-the-bot","title":"Install the bot","text":"<p>Generate an OAuth link with the command scopes and server permissions it needs.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#start-tutoring","title":"Start tutoring","text":"<p>Run the local process, then use mentions, direct messages, or <code>/tutor</code>.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#1-create-the-application","title":"1. Create the application","text":"<ol> <li>Open the Discord Developer Portal.</li> <li>Select New Application, enter a name such as <code>Data 8 Tutor</code>, and create    the application.</li> <li>Open Bot in the left sidebar. Discord creates the bot user for new    applications.</li> <li>Select Reset Token if necessary, then copy the bot token.</li> <li> <p>Add the token to the project <code>.env</code> file:</p> <p><code>dotenv DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=your-token</code></p> </li> </ol> <p>Warning</p> <p>Treat the bot token like a password. If it is exposed, reset it in the Developer Portal immediately.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#2-configure-messages-and-commands","title":"2. Configure messages and commands","text":"<p>On the Bot page, enable Message Content Intent and save. The <code>/tutor</code> slash command works without it, but the tutor needs this intent to read the question included with an <code>@mention</code>.</p> <p>Leave the Presence and Server Members intents disabled.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#3-install-the-bot","title":"3. Install the bot","text":"<ol> <li>Open OAuth2 -&gt; URL Generator.</li> <li>Enable these scopes:<ul> <li><code>bot</code></li> <li><code>applications.commands</code></li> </ul> </li> <li>Enable these bot permissions:<ul> <li>View Channels</li> <li>Send Messages</li> <li>Send Messages in Threads</li> <li>Read Message History</li> <li>Embed Links</li> <li>Use Slash Commands</li> </ul> </li> <li>Copy the generated URL and open it in a browser.</li> <li>Select your server and authorize the application.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#4-optional-sync-tutor-immediately","title":"4. Optional: sync <code>/tutor</code> immediately","text":"<p>Without a guild ID, Discord may take up to an hour to publish the global slash command. For immediate development sync:</p> <ol> <li>Enable Developer Mode under Discord User Settings -&gt; Advanced.</li> <li>Right-click the server name and select Copy Server ID.</li> <li> <p>Add it to <code>.env</code>:</p> <p><code>dotenv DISCORD_GUILD_ID=your-server-id</code></p> </li> </ol>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#5-run-the-tutor","title":"5. Run the tutor","text":"<pre><code>pip install -r requirements.txt\npython -m tutor ingest\npython -m tutor discord\n</code></pre> <p>Leave the process running. You should see a message indicating that the Discord tutor is ready.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#use-it-in-discord","title":"Use it in Discord","text":""},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#mention-the-tutor","title":"Mention the tutor","text":""},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/discord/#use-the-slash-command","title":"Use the slash command","text":"<ul> <li>Mention it: <code>@Data 8 Tutor how do I use tbl.where?</code></li> <li>Send the bot a direct message.</li> <li>Run <code>/tutor question: how do I use tbl.where?</code></li> </ul> <p>Stop the local process with Ctrl+C. The Discord interface always uses student mode and does not expose solution files.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/","title":"Grok Bot Tutor","text":"<p>Data 8 Tutor by Benjamin is a hosted Data 8 tutor for UC Berkeley students. On first use it sets up the Spring 2026 course materials on its own cloud computer. After that it gives hints, cites lectures and the textbook, and is instructed not to hand over solution code or answer keys.</p> <p>Unlike the self-hosted CLI, Discord, and Slack options, you do not install the tutor locally or import a course archive yourself.</p> <p>Add Data 8 Tutor to Grok Bot</p> <p></p> <p></p> <p></p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#add-the-template","title":"Add the template","text":"<p>Open the shared tutor and add a copy to your Grok Bot account.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#let-first-use-setup-finish","title":"Let first-use setup finish","text":"<p>The tutor downloads and stores Spring 2026 course materials on its own computer. You do not paste a setup prompt or provide local files.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#ask-a-data-8-question","title":"Ask a Data 8 question","text":"<p>Once setup completes, ask for hints and citations the same way you would ask a course tutor.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#what-you-need","title":"What you need","text":"<ul> <li>A Grok Bot account with an eligible paid plan</li> <li>Enough included or on-demand Grok Bot usage for first-use setup and later   questions</li> </ul>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#cost","title":"Cost","text":"<p>Grok Bot does not have a separate tutor fee. According to the Grok Bot FAQ, it is included with paid individual Cursor plans, Cursor Teams, and linked SuperGrok subscriptions. Eligible subscriptions include weekly usage; additional work may be billed on demand according to model and token cost.</p> <p>SuperGrok currently starts at $30 per month and lists Grok Bot access as an included feature. Prices, included usage, and account eligibility can change, so check the live pricing and usage pages before starting.</p> <p>First-use setup uses resources</p> <p>The tutor's automatic Spring 2026 setup can consume a meaningful part of the account's included Grok Bot usage. Later questions also consume usage.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#1-add-the-tutor","title":"1. Add the tutor","text":"<ol> <li>Open Data 8 Tutor by Benjamin.</li> <li>Select Add to Grok Bot.</li> <li>Review the template's instructions and integrations before accepting it.</li> <li>Open the new tutor's chat.</li> </ol> <p>The official Grok Bot FAQ states that adding a shared bot gives you a copy of its configuration and does not give the template author your computer, logins, or conversation history.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#2-let-it-set-up-the-course-materials","title":"2. Let it set up the course materials","text":"<p>On first use, the tutor sets up the Spring 2026 course materials on its own persistent cloud computer. Wait for that step to finish before asking course questions.</p> <p>Do not upload files from your personal computer, and do not ask the tutor to browse, mount, or access your local drives. The template is designed to collect and store the corpus itself.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#3-ask-a-question","title":"3. Ask a question","text":"<p>After setup completes, ask:</p> <pre><code>How do I use tbl.where? Give me a hint and cite the lecture or textbook\nsource you used.\n</code></pre> <p>A useful response should explain the concept, cite course material, and avoid providing assignment solution code.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#data-and-privacy-boundaries","title":"Data and privacy boundaries","text":"<p>The tutor stores course materials on a persistent cloud computer. The official FAQ notes that every bot on one account shares that computer, including its files and browser sessions. Separate bots are therefore not a security boundary.</p> <p>Course materials include restricted solution files</p> <p>The Spring 2026 corpus includes solution and answer-key files. The tutor is instructed not to disclose them, but prompt instructions are not a technical access control. Do not share the cloud-computer account with students, and do not rely on a separate bot to isolate those files.</p> <p>Grok Bot requires cloud data storage and does not support Legacy Privacy Mode. Review the account's privacy settings before adding the tutor.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/grok-bot/#course-material-licensing","title":"Course material licensing","text":"<p>The Spring 2026 materials retain their source licenses. Included content uses a mixture of BSD 3-Clause, CC BY-NC 4.0, and CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 terms. Use the corpus noncommercially, preserve attribution, and consult each included license before redistributing or modifying its materials.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/local-cli/","title":"Local CLI Tutor","text":"<p>The command-line interface is the fastest way to test the tutor without configuring a chat platform. First complete Data 8 Getting Started.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/local-cli/#use-it-in-the-local-cli","title":"Use it in the local CLI","text":""},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/local-cli/#one-question","title":"One question","text":"<pre><code>python -m tutor ask \"How do I use tbl.where?\"\n</code></pre> <p>The command retrieves relevant course chunks, generates one response, lists its source files, and exits.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/local-cli/#interactive-chat","title":"Interactive chat","text":"<pre><code>python -m tutor chat\n</code></pre> <p>The chat remembers recent turns so you can ask follow-up questions. Enter <code>/quit</code> to exit.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/local-cli/#inspect-retrieval","title":"Inspect retrieval","text":"<p>Use <code>retrieve</code> to see which course chunks match a question without calling the LLM:</p> <pre><code>python -m tutor retrieve \"How do I filter rows of a table?\"\n</code></pre> <p>Change the number of returned chunks with <code>-k</code>:</p> <pre><code>python -m tutor retrieve \"What is bootstrapping?\" -k 5\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/local-cli/#staff-mode","title":"Staff mode","text":"<p>Authorized course staff can include solution materials:</p> <pre><code>python -m tutor ask \"Explain this solution\" --staff\npython -m tutor chat --staff\n</code></pre> <p>Within interactive chat, <code>/staff</code> toggles staff mode. Discord and Slack always remain in student mode.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/","title":"Slack setup","text":"<p>The Slack tutor responds to mentions, direct messages, and the <code>/tutor</code> slash command. It uses Socket Mode, so you do not need a public HTTPS endpoint. Complete Data 8 Getting Started first.</p> <p></p> <p></p> <p></p> <p></p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#create-the-app","title":"Create the app","text":"<p>Register a Slack app in the workspace where learners will use the tutor.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#connect-securely","title":"Connect securely","text":"<p>Enable Socket Mode and keep the app-level and bot tokens in <code>.env</code>.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#configure-messages","title":"Configure messages","text":"<p>Add the required scopes, event subscriptions, direct messages, and <code>/tutor</code>.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#start-tutoring","title":"Start tutoring","text":"<p>Run the local process and invite the tutor into the appropriate channels.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#1-create-the-app","title":"1. Create the app","text":"<ol> <li>Open Slack API Apps.</li> <li>Select Create New App -&gt; From scratch.</li> <li>Name the app, for example <code>Data 8 Tutor</code>, and select a workspace.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#2-enable-socket-mode","title":"2. Enable Socket Mode","text":"<ol> <li>Open Socket Mode in the left sidebar and enable it.</li> <li>Create an app-level token named <code>socket</code> with the <code>connections:write</code> scope.</li> <li> <p>Copy the token beginning with <code>xapp-</code> into <code>.env</code>:</p> <p><code>dotenv SLACK_APP_TOKEN=your-app-token</code></p> </li> </ol>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#3-add-permissions-and-install","title":"3. Add permissions and install","text":"<ol> <li>Open OAuth &amp; Permissions.</li> <li>Under Bot Token Scopes, add:<ul> <li><code>app_mentions:read</code></li> <li><code>chat:write</code></li> <li><code>im:history</code></li> <li><code>im:read</code></li> <li><code>im:write</code></li> <li><code>commands</code></li> </ul> </li> <li>Select Install to Workspace and authorize the requested permissions.</li> <li> <p>Copy the Bot User OAuth Token beginning with <code>xoxb-</code> into <code>.env</code>:</p> <p><code>dotenv SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=your-bot-token</code></p> </li> </ol> <p>Warning</p> <p>Keep both Slack tokens in <code>.env</code>. If either token is exposed, rotate it in your Slack app settings.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#4-subscribe-to-messages","title":"4. Subscribe to messages","text":"<ol> <li>Open Event Subscriptions and enable events.</li> <li>Under Subscribe to bot events, add:<ul> <li><code>app_mention</code></li> <li><code>message.im</code></li> </ul> </li> <li>Save the event subscription settings.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#5-add-the-slash-command-and-messages-tab","title":"5. Add the slash command and messages tab","text":"<ol> <li>Open Slash Commands -&gt; Create New Command.</li> <li>Set the command to <code>/tutor</code>, the description to <code>Ask the Data 8 tutor</code>, and    the usage hint to <code>your question</code>.</li> <li>Open App Home. Under Show Tabs, enable Allow users to send Slash    commands and messages from the messages tab.</li> <li> <p>Add the bot to a channel:</p> <p><code>text /invite @Data 8 Tutor</code></p> </li> </ol> <p>Use the display name you chose if it is different.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#6-run-the-tutor","title":"6. Run the tutor","text":"<pre><code>pip install -r requirements.txt\npython -m tutor ingest\npython -m tutor slack\n</code></pre> <p>Leave the process running. You should see <code>Slack tutor starting (Socket Mode)</code>.</p>"},{"location":"tutor-options/interfaces/slack/#use-it-in-slack","title":"Use it in Slack","text":"<ul> <li>Mention it: <code>@Data 8 Tutor how do I use tbl.where?</code></li> <li>Send the app a direct message.</li> <li>Run <code>/tutor how do I use tbl.where?</code></li> </ul> <p>Stop the process with Ctrl+C. The Slack interface always uses student mode and does not expose solution files.</p>"}]}